) , which encodes context into a small set of trainable parameters (e.g., soft prompts or LoRA adapters). These parameters are plugged into a frozen LLM and optimized using context-related objectives, typically supervised next-token prediction or QA-style training. At inference time, the model can access the contextual information through these internal parameters, rather than explicitly attending to external context tokens. This enables long contexts to be incorporated beyond the model's context window limit, while substantially reducing inference cost since the input sequence only contains the task instruction."]
